{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'call-site occurrence|call-site-malformed|declared canonical|transport|Addendum A' docs\"","aggregated_output":"docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:93:  coordinate plus manifest address into a transportable exact-bounded d5\ndocs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:119:`dao.stream.rpc` already provides the transport shape this wants — a WebSocket server\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:2:description: Advanced concepts - parallel transport across dao.jing instances, capability tokens, entanglement/consensus\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:7:Parallel transport moves datoms from one dao.jing to another.\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:8:Entity-ref v values are local gauge; they must be resolved for transport.\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:19:Neither subsumes the other. Scope does not travel: transported datoms land in the\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:42:AST parallel transport (content-hash based):\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:53:Continuation parallel transport (serialization based):\ndocs/agents/advanced-concepts.md:64:  Content addressing and parallel transport are orthogonal mechanisms.\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:94:Half right. I/O host dependencies are behind v1's `open!` multimethod and `defopen` registry, with `#?(:clj … :cljs … :cljd …)` branches in every transport (file, log, udp, ws, http, transit, runtime driver). Only 10 files declare Java imports and 8 of them are dao.stream or dao.runtime. But most of the jolt failure taxonomy lands outside the stream layer:\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:131:The v2 plan explicitly defers file, UDP, log, relation and RPC transports and every consumer migration. `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.file`, `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.space` and `dao.runtime` (v1) still require legacy `dao.stream`, so the largest jolt error groups sit outside v2:\ndocs/jolt-experiment.md:138:Accurate statement: v2 has already reduced the stream-layer port to roughly three small files, but the storage and content-addressing layers have not moved onto v2 and still carry their own interop. When file and log transports land in v2, the same host-edge layout absorbs RandomAccessFile and the dht socket code. SHA-256, parallelSort and isFinite need a separate host shim in the style of `dao.data.arrays`, since they are not stream concerns and will never sit behind any dao.stream.\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:90:for nodes; it will not know the index was fetched from a peer.\" The network transport\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:108:transport** (`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`). Instead of `dao.space.query` pulling\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:118:this is continuation parallel transport\"), just applied here for locality/performance\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:123:boundary and the only goal is avoiding network chatter. Same transport primitive,\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:143:and continuation parallel transport is the single mechanism that serves both the\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:161:- `docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md` — parallel transport, including continuation\ndocs/ideas/sqllite.md:162:  parallel transport\ndocs/agents/team.md:39:| [Stream & Network](roles/stream-engineer.md)      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC                                                 |\ndocs/ideas/meerkat.md:102:\"A network content handle can be delivered today over the transports `datom.world`\ndocs/jolt-vs-jank.md:94:  library). Until it exists, ws transports simply report the design-doc's\ndocs/jolt-vs-jank.md:97:### M4 — File / log / UDP transports, then the storage layer (unlock)\ndocs/jolt-vs-jank.md:99:v2 deliberately deferred file, UDP, log, and RPC transports. When they land\ndocs/jolt-vs-jank.md:183:(jank's strength — the blocking queue stays); M4's transports bind POSIX\ndocs/ideas/mpi.md:56:   MPI is fundamentally transport-bound. `datom.world` scales from identical network semantics down to in-process sharing across heterogeneous runtimes using the same unified model. The \"zero-copy\" claim is scoped and worth stating precisely: what every runtime shares is the *`dao.jing` segment bytes* (immutable blobs each runtime reads lazily into its own datom representation, with no serialization round-trip for coordination state), not in-memory objects and not the bulk numerical payloads, which live in VRAM as handles outside `dao.space`. So this does not contest MPI's microsecond-kernel advantage (point 1 above, about moving raw buffers); it is a statement about coordination metadata spanning language boundaries cheaply, which MPI has no analog for at all.\ndocs/agents/roles/vm-engineer.md:10:- Serializable continuations, suspension, transport, and resumption\ndocs/agy-test.md:16:While high-level macro integration, basic stream transports, and query contracts are well-tested, a detailed inventory reveals several key areas with **zero direct unit coverage**, **thin helper coverage**, or **missing visual/partition-reconciliation testing**.\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:33:| DHT 64 KiB blob cap: `py` cannot travel over DHT | docs 7.5 | DHT seat | chunked transport | P2 |\ndocs/agents/known-gaps.md:67:| Closed (J4c): jolt `FileChannel.tryLock` failed, so file journals answered `:transport-error :defect :locked` (`dao.space.store.fs` fs.cljc:208-210) | J2 rerun 2; owner “both sets” 2026-10-23; J4b (locks) and J4c collab/1792992790877-engineer-j4c-journals-custody.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md | J4 (J4b, J4c) | closed: the journal's lock is J4b's fcntl `:jolt` arm and its content log J4a's, so the six whole-namespace exclusions (dao.stream.journal.file-test, yin.vm.ucf.durability-test, yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test, yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixture-test, yin.vm.ucf.compose-rows-test, yin.vm.ucf.compose-test) rejoined `test:jolt-core` and yin.vm.ucf.crash-cut-test's file subset was restored as before 9c2d8da0 (memory oracle untouched); J4c adds review F3's fail-closed handle state (after a torn append or a failed fsync the jolt content-log 